"explanation": "This question from Chapter 5.3 requires the student to identify a very specific and complex verb tense/aspect combination: the past perfect progressive.\n\nThe formula for the past perfect progressive is: **had + been + present participle (-ing form of the verb)**.\nThis aspect is used to emphasize the duration of an ongoing action that occurred *before another action or time in the past*.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\n- 'She <u>has been working</u> here for years.' This is 'has been + -ing', which is the Present Perfect Progressive (action started in past and continues to present).\n- 'The team <u>was celebrating</u> its victory.' This is 'was + -ing', which is the Past Progressive (ongoing action in the past).\n- 'He <u>had been waiting</u> for an hour when the bus finally arrived.' This is 'had been + -ing'. The action of waiting was ongoing and occurred before the past action of the bus arriving. This perfectly fits the structure and use of the Past Perfect Progressive.\n- 'They <u>will have finished</u> the project by Friday.' This is 'will have + past participle', which is the Future Perfect.\n\nThe difficulty lies in distinguishing this complex aspect from the similarly named present perfect progressive and past progressive."
