"explanation": "This question from Chapter 9.3 asks for the definition of 'voice' in writing and distinguishes it from the related concepts of tone and style.\n\n- **Style** is the combination of diction, syntax, etc.\n- **Tone** (Option B) is the writer's attitude toward the subject or audience (e.g., serious, humorous).\n- **Organization** (Option C) is about structure.\n- **Voice**, as the textbook describes, is the more holistic quality of the writing that reflects the writer's unique personality, perspective, and way of expressing themselves. It's what makes one writer's work sound different from another's, even if they are writing about the same topic. It is the 'person behind the words'.\n\nOption D is the best and most complete definition of voice. While vocabulary (part of style) and formality (part of tone/register) contribute to voice, voice itself is the overall impression of the writer's personality. The difficulty lies in separating 'voice' from the closely related 'tone'."
