"explanation": "This question from Chapter 1.4 requires a nuanced understanding of the challenges posed by the global spread of English. 'Mutual intelligibility' refers to the ability of speakers of different dialects or varieties of a language to understand each other.\n\n- While core grammar (Option A) can differ, it is often less of a barrier to basic understanding than pronunciation or vocabulary.\n- American slang (Option B) can cause confusion, but it is a relatively small part of the lexicon and often learned through media.\n- The ownership debate (Option D) is a sociolinguistic issue, not a direct barrier to understanding speech.\n- The most significant barrier to mutual intelligibility arises when the sound systems (phonology) and vocabularies (lexis) of different varieties diverge significantly. A speaker of Standard American English might struggle to understand a speaker of a strong Singaporean English ('Singlish') or Jamaican Patois, not because the underlying grammar is completely alien, but because the pronunciation, rhythm, intonation, and use of local vocabulary are so different. These differences in phonology and lexis are the primary hurdles to immediate comprehension between speakers of different World Englishes."
