"explanation": "This is a subtle phonology question from Chapter 2.3 that tests the concept of the 'linking r' and contrasts it with the behavior of rhotic speakers.\n\n- A rhotic speaker pronounces the /r/ sound wherever it appears in the spelling. So, they say 'car' as /kɑːr/ and 'car engine' as /kɑːr ˈɛndʒɪn/.\n- A non-rhotic speaker does not pronounce the /r/ at the end of a syllable or word. They say 'car' as /kɑː/. However, there is a special rule for connected speech: if a word that ends in a silent <r> is followed by a word that begins with a vowel, the /r/ sound is often pronounced to link the two words smoothly. This is called the linking r.\n\nSo, for 'car engine', the non-rhotic speaker, who would say /kɑː/, will re-introduce the /r/ sound to link to 'engine', resulting in a pronunciation very similar to the rhotic speaker: /kɑːr ˈɛndʒɪn/.\n\n- Option A is incorrect; an intrusive /j/ is not used here.\n- Option B is the opposite of what a rhotic speaker does.\n- Option D is incorrect because the underlying reason for the /r/ sound's presence is different for each speaker. But Option C describes the specific process for the non-rhotic speaker.\n\nThe difficulty lies in understanding this specific exception in non-rhotic speech patterns."
