"answer": "She was not only a great scientist but also a talented musician.",
"explanation": "This is a tricky syntax question (Chapter 5.5) focused on correlative conjunctions. The key to using them correctly is parallelism: the grammatical structure following the first part of the pair must be the same as the structure following the second part.\n\nLet's analyze the options:\n- Option D: 'either [to go to the movies] (infinitive phrase) or [the concert] (noun phrase)'. This is not parallel.\n- Option C: 'Neither [the students] (noun) nor [the teacher] (noun)'. The structure is parallel. However, there is a subject-verb agreement error. When subjects are joined by 'nor', the verb agrees with the closer subject ('teacher'). It should be 'was prepared', not 'were prepared'. So the sentence is grammatically flawed.\n- Option B: 'not only [a great scientist] (noun phrase), but [a talented musician also] (noun phrase with adverb at end)'. The placement of 'also' is awkward and disrupts the parallel flow compared to Option A.\n- Option A: 'not only [a great scientist] (noun phrase) but also [a talented musician] (noun phrase)'. The structure following 'not only' is a noun phrase, and the structure following 'but also' is a noun phrase. They are perfectly parallel. This is the most correct and well-formed sentence.\n\nThe difficulty lies in checking both the pairing of the conjunctions and the parallel structure of the elements they connect."
