"explanation": "This is a capstone question requiring a multi-level linguistic analysis to identify the primary mechanism of cohesion in a complex sentence. Cohesion (Chapter 7.2) is the grammatical and lexical linking that holds a text together.\n\nThe sentence is: 'The old book's cover, [which was made of faded leather], felt smooth.'\nThe key is understanding how the bracketed clause connects to the noun phrase 'The old book's cover'.\n\nThe connection is made by the word 'which'. 'Which' is a relative pronoun. Its syntactic function is to introduce a relative clause and link it back to its antecedent (the noun it refers to, which is 'The old book's cover'). This is a purely grammatical, or syntactic, link. The entire structure of the non-restrictive relative clause is a syntactic device for embedding additional information.\n\n- Phonological cohesion (alliteration, rhyme) is not present.\n- Morphological cohesion (shared affixes) is not the mechanism here.\n- Semantic cohesion (lexical relationships) exists (books can have leather covers), but it is a secondary relationship. The primary, explicit link that holds the sentence structure together is the syntactic function of the relative pronoun 'which'. Without it, the two parts would be disconnected grammatically.\n\nTherefore, the primary source of cohesion is at the syntactic level."
